dot 5 &= 0 \\\\\n10x + x^{2} + 25 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 10x + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 5)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=-5$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119622 1069 "e-4789" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119622 1071 "Find $s$, if $3\\mathbf{a_1}+s\\mathbf{a_2} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119622 1072 #uuid "fd3cb5b1-f16e-5a52-82e4-25bbbd1c4fe7" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119622 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $s$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a_{1} + sa_{2} &= b \\\\\n3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $s=4$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119623 1069 "e-4790" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119623 1071 "Find $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119623 1072 #uuid "548ef945-392b-5e75-9b60-14f4e2de6261" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119623 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2x &= b \\\\\n2x &= b - a \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}(b - a) \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119624 1069 "e-4791" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119624 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf d$, then what is $x_1+x_2$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119624 1072 #uuid "13ebde04-8518-5837-9420-c9487debc562" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119624 1074 "If\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf d\n$$\n\nthen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}4x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ -x_{1} \\\\ 3x_{1} + x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{4x_{1} + x_{2} = 5; - x_{1} =-5; 3x_{1} + x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $x_1=5$, which we substitute into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x_{1} + x_{2} &= 5 \\\\\n4(5) + x_{2} &= 5 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{1} + x_{2} &= 0 \\\\\n3(5) + (-15) &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement.\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$, which means\n\n$$\n5 \\mathbf{c_1}-15 \\mathbf{c_2} = \\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2=5-15=-10\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119625 1069 "e-4795" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119625 1071 "Determine if $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119625 1072 #uuid "d5a81b39-421e-55c3-90de-d9f4e6489cd5" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119625 1074 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 8; x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 2; 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} =-5\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} - 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=-\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 8R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} & ↔R_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = 3\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119626 1069 "e-4799" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119626 1071 "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119626 1072 #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119626 1074 "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsb + tc &= u \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; 4s + 4t = 10; 5s + 6t = 12\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 4R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} - R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; - 4t = 2; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 2t &= 2 \\\\\ns + 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) &= 2 \\\\\ns - 1 &= 2 \\\\\ns &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution\n\n$$\ns=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi$. In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119627 1069 "e-4807" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119627 1071 "The matrix $B$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(B)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$, where $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2$, and $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 15 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119627 1072 #uuid "244793f6-41c3-5920-9264-bec32cda9c9c" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119627 1074 "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(B)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}B \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}B & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 15 & -6 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}↔R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the true statement $0=0$. So the system is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 7; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=0$. Substituting into the first equation gives\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 7 5\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(B)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119628 1069 "e-4808" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119628 1071 "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(C)$. In affirmative case, find the value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3$, where $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}=x_1\\mathbf{c}_1+x_2\\mathbf{c}_3+x_3\\mathbf{c}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119628 1072 #uuid "12f222d8-5563-544a-8154-fb1f9406b231" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119628 1074 "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(C)$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}C \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}C & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 5; 3x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 1; 5x_{3} = 5\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3=1$. Substituting into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{2} + 4(1) &= 1 \\\\\n3x_{2} &= -3 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= 5 - x_{2} - 4x_{3} \\\\\n&= 5 - (-1) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation\n\n$$\nC\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(C)$.\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{c}_1$, $\\mathbf{c}_2$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 = -2\n$$\n\n." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119629 1069 "e-4810" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119629 1071 "The matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{b}_3$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119629 1072 #uuid "fe2f3934-fa5c-5a59-be7e-00b6e4889063" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119629 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb_{1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-27 \\\\ 45 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot \\\\\nAb_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = 0✓ \\\\\nAb_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\ne 0\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathbf{b}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119630 1069 "e-4812" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119630 1071 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119630 1072 #uuid "e38db5fa-1753-5176-bb5e-a077ea63ddec" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119630 1074 "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then we must have\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAb &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 + 2k - 12 \\\\ -6 + 3k - 3 \\\\ 12 - 6k + 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{2k - 6 = 0; 3k - 9 = 0; - 6k + 18 = 0\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119631 1069 "e-4814" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119631 1071 "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119631 1072 #uuid "1e59cd29-ea54-5b90-8215-60c81d1b6dbb" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119631 1074 "We need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 0; x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} - 3x_{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} - 3(0) &= 0 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{\\mathbf{0} \\right\\}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119632 1069 "e-4816" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119632 1071 "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ by inspection." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119632 1072 #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119632 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S$.\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2=1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_1+1\\cdot \\mathbf{a}_3\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector $\\mathbf{a}_2$ can be removed from $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ without changing the span. So\n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}\n$$\n\nis spanned by just $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$.\n\nLastly, note that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S$.\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_3\\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119633 1069 "e-4819" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119633 1071 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119633 1072 #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119633 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nSince $A\\sim B$, the equation\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4\n$$\n\nwhere $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} = x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}, x_{4} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119634 1069 "e-4820" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119634 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119634 1072 #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119634 1074 "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-x_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119635 1069 "e-4821" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119635 1071 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119635 1072 #uuid "a9d2ed82-2ba5-53a6-973d-c859ea5c295f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119635 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; x_{3} - 4x_{4} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}$. This implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119636 1069 "e-4822" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119636 1071 "Given the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119636 1072 #uuid "ef3be538-d974-5371-bd5b-a7e2ef3df827" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119636 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0} \\}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119637 1069 "e-4823" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119637 1071 "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119637 1072 #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119637 1074 "We know that any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}$. Since we are told that $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-1)_(⏟)_(x_{1})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + (3)_(⏟)_(x_{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119638 1069 "e-4824" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119638 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119638 1072 #uuid "6c7237cd-c655-575f-83ac-7a0891c336b2" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119638 1074 "We need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -11 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-3)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix above corresponds to the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} =-5; - 4x_{2} = 4\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2 =-1$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 + 3 \\left(-1 \\right) = -5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1 = -2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119639 1069 "e-4825" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119639 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119639 1072 #uuid "90f07e38-405a-5f79-8501-0b4dfb8fa4c8" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119639 1074 "We need to find $x_1$, $x_2$, and $x_3$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2} + x_{3}b_{3} &= x \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above system as an augmented matrix $M$ and reducing using Gaussian elimination, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{1}{3})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 1; - 9x_{2} - x_{3} = 7; \\frac{4}{3}x_{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 =2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-9x_2 - 2 = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_2=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+3(-1) = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_1 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119640 1069 "e-4827" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119640 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119640 1072 #uuid "6a082146-0d63-59dd-9224-a319d73ca59c" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119640 1074 "Since $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5b_{1} - 2b_{2} \\\\\n&=-2b_{2} + 5b_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(-b_{2}) - \\frac{5}{3}(-3b_{1}) \\\\\n&= 2c_{1} - \\frac{5}{3}c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119641 1069 "e-4828" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119641 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119641 1072 #uuid "f07356b8-aeb3-50ac-b017-c370d87395d2" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119641 1074 "Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2$, and $\\mathbf c_3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2b_{1} - 3b_{2} + 5b_{3} \\\\\n&=-3b_{2} + 5b_{3} - 2b_{1} \\\\\n&= (-3b_{2}) - 5(-b_{3}) + \\frac{1}{2}(-4b_{1}) \\\\\n&= c_{1} - 5c_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119642 1069 "e-4829" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119642 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119642 1072 #uuid "91fac4cd-7097-5244-a385-9f3f76395912" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119642 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{c_{1} = b_{2} - b_{1}; c_{2} = 2b_{1} - 3b_{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left(-2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nSince $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= b_{1} - 3b_{2} \\\\\n&= (-3c_{1} - c_{2}) - 3(-2c_{1} - c_{2}) \\\\\n&=-3c_{1} - c_{2} + 6c_{1} + 3c_{2} \\\\\n&= 3c_{1} + 2c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119643 1069 "e-4910" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119643 1071 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}$. Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119643 1072 #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119643 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119644 1069 "e-4911" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119644 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n${x = 1 - 2t; y = 4t; z =-2 + 3t$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$. Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119644 1072 #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119644 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 - 2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2 + 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119645 1069 "e-4912" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119645 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/79/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119645 1072 #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119645 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨5,-1, 0⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6,-2, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-(-1)}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z-0}{0} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component is always maintains the constant value $z=0$. For example, our two points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nboth have $z$-coordinates of $z=0$.\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $z=0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2},\\; z=0\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119646 1069 "e-4932" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119646 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(-3,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 0, 2, 0 \\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119646 1072 #uuid "ba897e22-8104-5772-a0d3-9b062f3592f1" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119646 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nis normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - p \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119647 1069 "e-4933" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119647 1071 "A nonzero vector $\\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c} \\rangle$ is parallel to the plane $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119647 1072 #uuid "a33f6c43-4e25-5d67-9f73-ca99a18fb434" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119647 1074 "We need a vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nthat is parallel to the given plane.\n\nFrom the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle = 3\n$$\n\nof the plane, we get the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t_{1}, t_{2}, t_{3}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 4⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2t_{1} - t_{2} + 4t_{3} &= 0 \\\\\nt_{2} &= 2t_{1} + 4t_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0$. So, we must set $t_1=0$.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any\n\n$$\nt_3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFor example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119648 1069 "e-4935" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119648 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(\\sqrt{2},0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n${x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119648 1072 #uuid "43586a83-879c-5caf-9063-a8f4afbb8b4c" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119648 1074 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{q}=\\langle \\sqrt{2},0,-5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nConverting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{x = 3; y =-1 + \\sqrt{3}t; z = 5 - t\n$$\n\nThe direction vector of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nso a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 0, \\sqrt{3}, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - q) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n - q \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= q \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= ⟨\\sqrt{2}, 0,-5⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨0, \\sqrt{3},-1⟩ &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119649 1069 "e-4936" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119649 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119649 1072 #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119649 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= ⟨9,-2, 3⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-4, 7⟩ &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z &= 56 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 7z - 56 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119650 1069 "e-4937" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119650 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119650 1072 #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119650 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFrom the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle$. So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= ⟨ - 1, 4, 8⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z - 33 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1069 "e-4938" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi$, find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1072 #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1074 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y + z + 13 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -2x + y - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-13$. So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi$. Note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n(r - ⟨0, 0,-13⟩) \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 1⟩ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1069 "e-4939" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1072 #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v_{1} \\cdot v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-16i - 4j - 14k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z - 124 &= 0 \\\\\n8x + 2y + 7z + 62 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119653 1069 "e-4940" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119653 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119653 1072 #uuid "a6fded96-287a-53ee-8b1c-dd618562b2c2" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119653 1074 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= ⟨ - 3, 2, 5⟩ + s⟨17, 3, 15⟩ + t⟨5, 0,-8⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119654 1069 "e-4941" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119654 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(2,50,-19)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 2,\\sqrt{6},17 \\rangle + s \\langle -1,7,2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{r}= \\langle 20,-44, 27 \\rangle + t \\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119654 1072 #uuid "297bda8f-6e33-5cc2-a2e1-5ab6186fa9d0" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119654 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -1,7,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle\n$$\n\n, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= c + sv_{1} + tv_{2} \\\\\nr &= ⟨2, 50,-19⟩ + s⟨ - 1, 7, 2⟩ + t⟨2\\sqrt{3}, 9,-8⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119655 1069 "e-4942" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119655 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(-1,4,-3)$, $B(7,5,4)$, and $C(2,-2,8)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119655 1072 #uuid "9c36ddd5-fcf1-5c4d-a350-aa8ef3a3ca9e" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119655 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= ⟨7, 5, 4⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨8, 1, 7⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= ⟨2,-2, 8⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-6, 11⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -1,4,-3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nof the point $A$, then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= a + sAB^(→) + tAC^(→) \\\\\nr &= ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ + s⟨8, 1, 7⟩ + t⟨3,-6, 11⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The form of the final equation depends on our choice of vectors and the point on the plane. We could have picked other vectors. Likewise, we could have chosen $B$ or $C$ instead of $A$ as our point on the plane. So, the answer is *not unique*!" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119656 1069 "e-4943" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119656 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n$l: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 5,3,-4 \\rangle + t \\langle 4,-2,-3 \\rangle, \\qquad \\Pi: \\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle -7,5,1 \\rangle = -65$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119656 1072 #uuid "1d809050-9477-5de6-8c95-7294804e7b69" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119656 1074 "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 5+4t, 3-2t, -4-3t \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 5+4t, 3-2t, -4-3t \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 7, 5, 1⟩ &= -65 \\\\\n⟨5 + 4t, 3 - 2t,-4 - 3t⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 7, 5, 1⟩ &= -65 \\\\\n- 7(5 + 4t) + 5(3 - 2t) + (-4 - 3t) &= -65 \\\\\n- 35 - 28t + 15 - 10t - 4 - 3t &= -65 \\\\\n- 41t &= -41 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point is obtained by substituting $t=1$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= ⟨5 + 4(1), 3 - 2(1),-4 - 3(1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨9, 1,-7⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(9, 1, -7)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1069 "e-4944" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n$l: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1,3,1 \\rangle + t \\langle 2,1,0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\Pi:4x-3y+2z-7 = 0$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1072 #uuid "c1576b30-9142-5884-b654-7ade7a765ec7" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t, 3+t, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 3y + 2z - 7 &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 3y + 2z &= 7 \\\\\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t, 3+t, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\n⟨1 + 2t, 3 + t, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\n4(1 + 2t) - 3(3 + t) + 2 \\cdot 1 &= 7 \\\\\n4 + 8t - 9 - 3t + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5t &= 10 \\\\\nt &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= ⟨1 + 2(2), 3 + (2), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 5, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5, 5, 1)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119658 1069 "e-4945" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119658 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n${x = 3 - 4t; y =-1 + 2t; z = 2 + 3t$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119658 1072 #uuid "f952e670-a673-5c15-913b-c396e8885142" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119658 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y + 6z + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n3(3 - 4t) + 4(-1 + 2t) + 6(2 + 3t) + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 12t - 4 + 8t + 12 + 18t + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n14t &= -42 \\\\\nt &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{x = 3 - 4(-3) = 15; y =-1 + 2(-3) =-7; z = 2 + 3(-3) =-7\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(15,-7,-7)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1069 "e-4949" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1072 #uuid "38fce106-2ba8-554c-ace3-a5731d15a635" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid (-1) \\cdot 2 + 6 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot (-4)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2} + (-4)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid - 2 + 24 - 16 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 16 + 16}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 6\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1069 "e-4950" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1071 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4$, find the shortest distance from $P(0, -1, 2)$ to $\\Pi$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1072 #uuid "74693316-2e5a-5821-87a5-fdc5b8719c82" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨1,-2,-1⟩ - ⟨0,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 0 + (-3) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 0 + 6 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 0 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 8\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1069 "e-4951" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3, -10, 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1072 #uuid "1538dde4-ab62-502e-8efe-7f37628ab861" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z$. Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y - 2z &= -1 \\\\\n2(4) + (-5) + 1 &= 2z \\\\\n4 &= 2z \\\\\nz &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨4,-5, 2⟩ - ⟨3,-10, 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 5,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane\n\n$$\nax+by+cz=d\n$$\n\nthe normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, for the plane $\\Pi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 1^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 5 + 2 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 9\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1069 "e-5021" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1072 #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 4t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 + 5t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $y=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119663 1069 "e-5182" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119663 1071 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119663 1072 #uuid "5cc1443c-03cd-5f86-bcfc-b9b7f4c6a565" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119663 1074 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &= (-6) \\cdot C_{41} + 0 \\cdot C_{42} + 0 \\cdot C_{43} + 1 \\cdot C_{44} \\\\\n&= (-6) \\cdot (-1)^{4 + 1} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot (-1)^{4 + 4} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1069 "e-5230" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}$\nExpress $D_x$ in terms of a $2 \\times 2$ determinant." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1072 #uuid "d81e32a1-bd93-5a95-bb4a-e8df9063b5aa" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{x} &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1069 "e-5410" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1072 #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119666 1069 "e-5411" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119666 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119666 1072 #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119666 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1069 "e-5415" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1071 "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1072 #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1074 "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$.\n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is\n$7-3=4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=4\n$$\n\n." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1069 "e-5417" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1071 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1072 #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1074 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows.\nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(C)=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1069 "e-5418" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1072 #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1074 "To find the rank of $A$, we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 6R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119670 1069 "e-5884" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119670 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n$\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119670 1072 #uuid "b86eace4-01cb-5601-8e9b-2658e81fc656" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119670 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nare parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v \\cdot w \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\mid - 3 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&= i - 12j - 20k \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-12,-20⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1069 "e-6143" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1072 #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1074 "Since\n\n$$\n\\det(X)=\\det(Y)\n$$\n\n, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form\n\n$$\nR_j:= R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\ =\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= - 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= det(Y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1069 "e-6148" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5$. What is the value of $\\det(2A)$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1072 #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119672 1074 "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k$, and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\ndet(k \\cdot A) = k^{n} \\cdot det(A)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(2A) &= 2^{4} \\cdot det(A) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1069 "e-6172" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1071 "Consider the system of equations\n${9x + 3y - 6z = 8; - 6x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x - 5y - 15z = 10$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1072 #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119673 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{9x + 3y + (-6)z = 8; (-6)x + 4y + 2z = 3; 5x + (-5)y + (-15)z = 10\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -5 & -15\\end{vmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1069 "e-6184" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1072 #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119674 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A$, we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although the left matrix is a lower triangular matrix, it is not a *unit* lower triangular matrix because it does not have $1$'s on the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1069 "e-6191" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b$, then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1072 #uuid "d9beff9d-db58-540f-811e-a07fb77d3d92" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119675 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ 2x_{3} \\\\ 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} + x_{3} \\\\ x_{1} + 2x_{3} \\\\ -x_{2} + 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; - x_{2} + 3x_{3} = 7\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{3} = 2\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1069 "e-6208" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1072 #uuid "b6d52e39-a8a0-5048-80a1-a342a2617e0f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119676 1074 "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, then the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nmust be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}A \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}A & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 + k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{8}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k - 48\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 11R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 10; x_{2} = 4; 0 = k - 4\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-4$, which means that $k=4$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119677 1069 "e-6218" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119677 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119677 1072 #uuid "21a6808a-5b1c-5b1d-9bd4-da605fb6d920" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119677 1074 "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 13; 5x_{1} + 4x_{2} =-5; 7x_{1} + 2x_{2} = k\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 5R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 7R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{14}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 16R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = k-11\n$$\n\n. Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0 = k-11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k=11\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119678 1069 "e-6237" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119678 1071 "Consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a basis of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119678 1072 #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119678 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{5}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$. So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$, that is to say,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119679 1069 "e-6257" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119679 1071 "The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ are given below. Determine whether $\\mathcal{B} = \\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3, \\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. If that is the case, find $x_1 + x_2 + x_3$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119679 1072 #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119679 1074 "We want to determine if $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$. So, we want to find $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + x_{3} = 2; - x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} =-2; - 3x_{1} - 2x_{3} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, $\\mathcal{B}$ is indeed a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3,\\mathbf{a}_4 \\}$.\nAlso, the matrix tells us that $x_1=-5$, $x_2=0$, and $x_3=7$, meaning that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 + x_3 = (-5) + 0 + 7 = 2\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119680 1069 "e-6282" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119680 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119680 1072 #uuid "a79126f2-e26c-5929-93d8-825d85b9a438" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119680 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = (-5) \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_1 + 2 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119681 1069 "e-6303" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119681 1071 "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119681 1072 #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119681 1074 "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (-\\sin t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{0}^{2π}‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\sqrt{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sqrt{2}t\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}(2π - 0) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119682 1069 "e-6804" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119682 1071 "Given that the area of a circle is $22\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, what is its radius?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119682 1072 #uuid "78a454c8-c08b-5c4a-8ff6-cd093296c6d9" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119682 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\n, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n22π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n22π &= πr^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= 22 \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{22}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119683 1069 "e-71" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119683 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119683 1072 #uuid "7a33db07-63f3-5f8a-b2cd-ca662b91e063" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119683 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3} = \\dfrac{(-3)^2-9}{(-3)+3} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} - 9}{x + 3} &= \\frac{x^{2} - 3^{2}}{x + 3} \\\\\n&= ((x + 3)(x - 3))/(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 3)\\frac{x^{2} - 9}{x + 3} &= lim_(x → - 3)(x - 3) \\\\\n&=-3 - 3 \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119684 1069 "e-7188" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119684 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the hole in the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{(x+6)(5x-2)}{(5x-2)(x+2)}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119684 1072 #uuid "ef03b842-54ac-5db9-a33a-6309e7e33588" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119684 1074 "To find the holes of a rational function, we first factor the numerator and denominator. Then, we identify the common factors in the numerator and denominator. Finally, we set these factors equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nIn this case, both the numerator and denominator of the function $f(x)$ are factored, and we notice that they both have $(5x-2)$ as a common factor.\n\nNow, we set the common factor equal to zero and solve for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\n5x-2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=\\dfrac 2 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a hole at\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac 2 5\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119685 1069 "e-7189" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119685 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the hole in the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-2}{x^2-1}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119685 1072 #uuid "abebd5c2-72cb-55de-9425-bdc90cfba8c9" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119685 1074 "To find the holes of a rational function, we first factor the numerator and denominator. Then, we identify the common factors in the numerator and denominator. Finally, we set these factors equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - x - 2}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((x + 1)(x - 2))/((x - 1)(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that the numerator and denominator both have $(x+1)$ as a common factor.\n\nNow, we set the common factor equal to zero and solve for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\nx + 1 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x= -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a hole at $x = -1$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119686 1069 "e-72" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119686 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119686 1072 #uuid "81aac71f-0559-51f5-9567-928d99c08c77" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119686 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1} = \\dfrac{1^3-2(1)^2+1}{1-1} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x}{x - 1} &= (x(x^{2} - 2x + 1))/(x - 1) \\\\\n&= (x(x - 1)^{2})/(x - 1) \\\\\n&= x(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x}{x - 1} &= lim_(x → 1)x(x - 1) \\\\\n&= 1(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119687 1069 "e-73" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119687 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119687 1072 #uuid "0c0f5ce6-a84b-51a8-ac60-c557966ec85c" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119687 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30} = \\dfrac{6^2-6\\cdot 6}{6^2-6-30} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} - 6x}{x^{2} - x - 30} &= (x(x - 6))/((x - 6)(x + 5)) = \\frac{x}{x + 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 6)\\frac{x^{2} - 6x}{x^{2} - x - 30} &= lim_(x → 6)\\frac{x}{x + 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{6 + 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119688 1069 "e-7678" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119688 1071 "Simplify $\\left(5.1 \\times 10^{3}\\right) + \\left(2 \\times 10^{3}\\right)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119688 1072 #uuid "6b7f821e-936e-5d8d-bf6d-3a09dc4ad68f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119688 1074 "Since the numbers have the same power of $10$, we add the decimal parts and then multiply the result by $10^{3}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5.1 \\cdot 10^{3}) + (2 \\cdot 10^{3}) &= (5.1 + 2) \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.1 \\cdot 10^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119689 1069 "e-7692" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119689 1071 "A rectangular prism is $6\\,\\textrm{in}$ long, $2\\,\\textrm{in}$ wide, and $4\\,\\textrm{in}$ high. What is the length of a diagonal of the solid?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119689 1072 #uuid "341b7455-06ec-5c85-9f04-290ab8648427" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119689 1074 "First, let's sketch the rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/89/89f8cfc66797f5a5625d17cb9178cae9a41e0049e6b3b717b1bd89aaa8dacd8f.png)\n\nWe can find the length of a diagonal $d$ in a rectangular solid using the formula\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting the values\n\n$$\nl=6\\,\\textrm{in}, \\qquad w=2\\,\\textrm{in}, \\qquad h=4\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for $d$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{6^{2} + 2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36 + 4 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{56} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{14}in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119690 1069 "e-7694" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119690 1071 "Consider the rectangular solid below. Calculate the value of $h$.\n\n![](images/e7/e7254df72dce76e357802a327d3153eaf8a62bd2c03eab76f582be31252c4859.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119690 1072 #uuid "73b35d62-480c-5710-8986-33c52dded509" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119690 1074 "We can find the length of a diagonal $d$ in a rectangular solid using the formula\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting the values\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{14}, \\qquad l=3\\,\\textrm{m}, \\qquad w=1\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for $d$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{14} &= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 1^{2} + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 1 + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{10 + h^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, solving for $h$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{14})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{10 + h^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n14 &= 10 + h^{2} \\\\\n4 &= h^{2} \\\\\nh &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119691 1069 "e-7695" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119691 1071 "Find the measure of the face diagonal $d$ of the rectangular solid below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbcc3de680edd6059d592292396d7cca8eed985d3d7cae0a8c6b384a545303.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119691 1072 #uuid "bd4f211e-061c-5a17-a300-5c2993756528" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119691 1074 "According to the diagram, $d$ is a diagonal of the solid's front face. The front face is a rectangle whose dimensions correspond to the length $l$ and the height $h$ of the prism, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f28d297201deae416107f4db6888cceab420fc837877675a7a18f994e5b0ee3d.png)\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{l^{2} + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{20} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119692 1069 "e-7707" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119692 1071 "Express $\\left(7.8 \\times 10^{-12} \\right) + \\left(5 \\times 10^{-12} \\right)$ in scientific notation." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119692 1072 #uuid "a4bc87fc-721b-56f9-98bb-1d2e07267e9e" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119692 1074 "Since the numbers have the same power of $10$, we add the decimal parts and then multiply the result by $10^{-12}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(7.8 \\cdot 10^{-12}) + (5 \\cdot 10^{-12}) &= (7.8 + 5) \\cdot 10^{-12} \\\\\n&= 12.8 \\cdot 10^{-12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above number is not in scientific notation because $12.8 \\gt 10$. So, we need to write $12.8$ in scientific notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n12.8 = 1.28\\times 10^1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12.8 \\cdot 10^{-12} &= (1.28 \\cdot 10^{1}) \\cdot 10^{-12} \\\\\n&= 1.28 \\cdot (10^{1} \\cdot 10^{-12}) \\\\\n&= 1.28 \\cdot 10^{1 - 12} \\\\\n&= 1.28 \\cdot 10^{-11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final answer is $1.28 \\times 10^{-11}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119693 1069 "e-8005" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119693 1071 "Calculate the value of\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119693 1072 #uuid "85acf106-2f03-5509-8ad0-08b82ef2a4c6" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119693 1074 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{2}:=C_{2} + 2C_{1}; C_{3}:=C_{3} + 3C_{1}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + (-3)C_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{3 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4(11 - 3); = 32\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119694 1069 "e-8024" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119694 1071 "Find the coordinates of the hole in the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{(x+5)(x+2)}{(x+2)(x+1)}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119694 1072 #uuid "112f22d8-96c2-5b9d-a08a-c7d3b63f7000" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119694 1074 "To find the holes of a rational function, we first factor the numerator and denominator. Then, we identify the common factors in the numerator and denominator. Finally, we set these factors equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nIn this case, both the numerator and denominator of the function $f(x)$ are factored, and we notice that they both have $(x+2)$ as a common factor.\n\nNow, we set the common factor equal to zero and solve for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\nx + 2 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a hole at $x = -2$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the hole, we find the reduced rational function $F(x)$ and evaluate it at the $x$-coordinate of the hole.\n\nFinding the reduced rational function $F(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= ((x + 5)(x + 2))/((x + 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 5)(x + 2))/((x + 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 5}{x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the hole is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2) &= \\frac{-2 + 5}{-2 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{-1} \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of the hole are $(-2,-3)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119695 1069 "e-8026" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119695 1071 "![](images/f1/f1e2a6a66f37b78374207a6ef31d3856cba3f8176664177abefb957e53e123e1.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown above, where $f(x)$ is the rational function given by\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{x(x-4)}{g(x)}$.\n\nWhich of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n1. $x+4$\n2. $x-4$\n3. $x$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119695 1072 #uuid "0c4c8aa3-0ac1-5782-b3bf-7a01b59e0be8" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119695 1074 "First, notice that the function has a hole at $x=4$.\n\nThe straight line (without the hole) has a slope of $1$ and a $y$-intercept of $0$. Therefore, its equation is\n$y = x$.\n\nTo introduce a hole at $x=4$, we need the function to have a common factor in the numerator and denominator that evaluates to zero when $x=4$. Therefore, from the given options, we must have\n$g(x) = x-4$.\n\nThe complete expression for $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x(x-4)}{x-4}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119696 1069 "e-8054" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119696 1071 "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119696 1072 #uuid "4a9954b2-3d4b-5669-88cd-6e74c9683a60" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119696 1074 "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1}; R_{4}:=R_{4} + 3R_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119697 1069 "e-8060" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119697 1071 "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119697 1072 #uuid "ccf558b9-bf00-58c7-b244-2c5797f93d8e" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119697 1074 "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{1}:=C_{1} + C_{3}; C_{2}:=C_{2} + (-2)C_{3}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + 3C_{3}; =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = -\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119698 1069 "e-8203" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119698 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(2,2^2)$, compute $P(X < 1.24)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119698 1072 #uuid "adabb3ab-fc9c-5857-95ac-008bc560b257" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119698 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < x) = P \\left(Z < \\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 2$ and $\\sigma = 2$.\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1.24) &= P(Z < \\frac{1.24 - 2}{2}) \\\\\n&= P(Z < - 0.38) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le - 0.38) \\\\\n&= Φ(-0.38)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48d92806543cb86b2906032b1723fa942db842e7f283681cafeeb9b11fca8cab.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.38) = 0.3520\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X < 1.24) = 0.3520\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119699 1069 "e-8204" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119699 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y \\sim N(5,4^2)$, compute $P(Y \\geq 2.92)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2743 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3085 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3050 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3015 \\,$ |" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119699 1072 #uuid "dad97320-cddd-501a-81bd-371090cc5be5" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119699 1074 "Given a normal random variable $Y \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq y) = P \\left(Z \\geq \\dfrac{y-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 5$ and $\\sigma = 4$.\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y \\ge 2.92) &= P(Z \\ge \\frac{2.92 - 5}{4}) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge - 0.52)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/04/049e4733082674668ebbe2c5a710cd847f1db16d26fdcd2af6e603941efdffe7.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 0.52) &= 1 - P(Z < - 0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le - 0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-0.52)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.52) =0.3015\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 0.52) &= 1 - Φ(-0.52) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.3015 \\\\\n&= 0.6985\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq 2.92) = 0.6985\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119700 1069 "e-8205" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119700 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(1,9)$, compute $P(-1.13 < X < 3.25)$.\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, -0.9 \\,$ $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ $\\, -0.8 \\,$ $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ $\\, -0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.03 \\,$$\\, 0.04 \\,$$\\, 0.05 \\,$$\\, 0.5 \\,$ $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ $\\, 0.6 \\,$ $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ $\\, 0.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119700 1072 #uuid "ab98835e-cf10-5fd3-b42b-fa50d8d95967" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119700 1074 "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a < X < b) = P \\left(\\dfrac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} < Z < \\dfrac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 1$ and\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt 9 = 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-1.13 < X < 3.25) &= P(\\frac{-1.13 - 1}{3} < Z < \\frac{3.25 - 1}{3}) \\\\\n&= P(-0.71 < Z < 0.75)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a50513f550d8a3122e2f2276883a0189fe9bdc6dd19159e8ae94dbd5b247f5a9.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.71 < Z < 0.75) &= P(Z < 0.75) - P(Z \\le - 0.71) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.75) - P(Z \\le - 0.71) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.75) - Φ(-0.71)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.71) &= 0.2389 \\\\\nΦ(0.75) &= 0.7734\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.71 < Z < 0.75) &= Φ(0.75) - Φ(-0.71) \\\\\n&= 0.7734 - 0.2389 \\\\\n&= 0.5345\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-1.13< X < 3.25) = 0.5345\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119701 1069 "e-84" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119701 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-16}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119701 1072 #uuid "d462c4fb-c784-5dc3-adc1-1cd562c6a55e" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119701 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 16} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x^{2} - 4^{2})) \\\\\n&= (1)/((x + 4)(x - 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119702 1069 "e-85" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119702 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-5x+6}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119702 1072 #uuid "ace1e5ac-fecc-559d-923d-9fec38f410ea" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119702 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} &= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-3 = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=3$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119703 1069 "e-86" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119703 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119703 1072 #uuid "43990279-e10b-53dc-8f18-533f3962a935" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119703 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2x + 2} &= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x(x + 1))/(2(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1$.\n\n![](images/ed/eda5457edafe14fb51fcc75ca75677b199bfc3e0aef8940c66f8aa78cf8cd5c3.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119704 1069 "e-8688" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119704 1071 "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\mathbf f(t) + \\langle 1,\\:-1,\\:1\\rangle\\,\\textrm{d}t$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle\\int_2^0\\mathbf f(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t= \\left\\langle -3, \\: 2, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119704 1072 #uuid "40927582-03c4-5dcb-b2cc-7c68c73388b1" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119704 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}f(t)dt &= -\\int_{2}^{0}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= - ⟨ - 3, 2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}f(t) + ⟨1,-1, 1⟩dt &= \\int_{0}^{2}f(t)dt + \\int_{0}^{2}⟨1,-1, 1⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩ + \\left.⟨t,-t, t⟩\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2,-1⟩ + ⟨2,-2, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5,-4, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119705 1069 "e-87" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119705 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+\\dfrac{1}{x+1}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119705 1072 #uuid "3b1c0748-807c-5a77-b9e8-62ce5eb03c78" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119705 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x + 1} &= ((x + 1))/(x(x + 1)) + (x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (x + 1 + x)/(x(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)/(x(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119706 1069 "e-8875" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119706 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119706 1072 #uuid "604fcbbb-6d34-5dca-8138-2dc99d524115" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119706 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119707 1069 "e-8876" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119707 1071 "$\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^2z-2y+z=6$.\nWhat is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119707 1072 #uuid "a1aa679a-bbe1-5682-bca4-5103f5717be0" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119707 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}z - 2y + z &= 6 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 1)z &= 6 + 2y \\\\\nz &= (2(y + 3))/(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v$, we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119708 1069 "e-8877" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119708 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0$. What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119708 1072 #uuid "d298fa26-3b8f-5561-944c-ae6c919867b4" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119708 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form\n\n$$\nz=f(r,\\theta)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} - 4z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n4z^{2} &= r^{2} \\\\\nz^{2} &= \\frac{r^{2}}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{r}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that\n\n$$\nz\\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac r2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black} \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black} \\dfrac r2}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119709 1069 "e-8878" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119709 1071 "$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}$.\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119709 1072 #uuid "d8feb600-a06a-581d-bd23-308c791d03e9" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119709 1074 "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨θ\\cos θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin θ\\sin^{2} ϕ, θ\\sin ϕ\\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119710 1069 "e-8924" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119710 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119710 1072 #uuid "763fad5f-7cb7-5ff3-a14e-e2849e90906a" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119710 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -26\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u_{33}=6$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119711 1069 "e-8925" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119711 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{31} \\cdot u_{23}$?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119711 1072 #uuid "b19414c3-443f-5075-a2db-f27eb3a7e936" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119711 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U$, as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9$, we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119712 1069 "e-8926" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119712 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n${Ly = b; Ux = y$\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119712 1072 #uuid "cbefba9a-eda2-5a8e-8bb5-59ea4175b835" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119712 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU$, we can write\n\n$$\nAx = b⟹(LU)_(⏟)_(A)x = b\n$$\n\nNow, setting\n\n$$\nU\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{Ly = b; Ux = y\n$$\n\nWe now solve\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = 5; 15y_{1} + y_{2} = 66\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9$.\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119713 1069 "e-8943" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119713 1071 "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: v^2, \\: u+v \\big\\rangle$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119713 1072 #uuid "175c458d-36cc-5f90-b6ee-976e082c30a2" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119713 1074 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$, the fundamental vector product is defined as $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$.\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u + v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2u, 0, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v^{2}), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u + v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 2v, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2v,-2u, 4uv⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119714 1069 "e-8947" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119714 1071 "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations\n$x = u, \\quad y = v, \\quad z = 1 - u - v$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119714 1072 #uuid "1b4b69f7-fc4f-57c3-bcaf-9df98e4fc5e3" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119714 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(1 - u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(1 - u - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ &= ‖⟨1, 1, 1⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 1 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{3}dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot Area(D) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119715 1069 "e-8958" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119715 1071 "$AAAAAAAA$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2$. What is the missing expression?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119715 1072 #uuid "4b202b4e-d8dc-524c-8cb4-ab0c00eb78cb" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119715 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D$, the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D \\left\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\| \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{3} - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0, 3u^{2}⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{3} - v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3u^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3u^{2}, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖ &= ‖⟨ - 3u^{2}, 1, 1⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3u^{2})^{2} + (1)^{2} + (1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9u^{4} + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)dS &= ∬_(D)‖r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}‖dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\sqrt{9u^{4} + 2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119716 1069 "e-8972" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119716 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $5 \\: \\textrm{m}$, $\\sqrt3 \\: \\textrm{m}$, and $\\sqrt6 \\: \\textrm{m}$, respectively?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119716 1072 #uuid "78d0bc32-ce61-5c58-a31a-bf3b0cc55761" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119716 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl=5 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad w=\\sqrt3 \\, \\textrm{m}, \\qquad h=\\sqrt6 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt3 \\cdot \\sqrt6 = 5\\sqrt{18} = 15\\sqrt2 \\: \\textrm{m}^3\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119717 1069 "e-8973" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119717 1071 "Determine the height of the rectangular solid below given that its volume is $700\\:\\textrm{cm}^3$.\n\n![](images/8a/8a232f69c9c6abbffcb2272d4fbd237c644273826fb46dd5742bdc07d89544cb.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119717 1072 #uuid "35043b46-40e9-51c4-8676-ad7a81763aae" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119717 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 700, \\qquad l=14, \\qquad w=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n700 &= 14 \\cdot 10 \\cdot h \\\\\n700 &= 140h \\\\\nh &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $5\\:\\textrm{cm}$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119718 1069 "e-8974" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119718 1071 "The diagram below shows a carton of orange juice. One-third of the carton is filled with juice, forming a cube. Find the volume of the empty space in the carton.\n\n![](images/90/90280dd78d285df5dc798c3ec90b7fb47a72414f2934d36885eb9d65fd7584fd.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119718 1072 #uuid "3528ee00-01a7-5c77-af81-50d53b5fa849" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119718 1074 "The orange juice in the carton creates a cube. Since the height of the juice is $9\\,\\textrm{cm}$, the side length of the cube is also $9\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nThe height of $9 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents a third of the carton's height. Therefore, the height of the carton is\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 9 = 27 \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the carton is a rectangular prism that is $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ long, $9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ wide, and\n\n$$\n27 - 9 = 18\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nhigh.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=9\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=18\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = 9 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 18 = 1\\,458\\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is $1\\,458 \\: \\textrm{cm}^3$ of empty space in the carton." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119719 1069 "e-8987" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119719 1071 "![](images/de/de6f981c4508644a6935abb03a5bcc1d78b9bb19d0431c0686b0705cf5507e36.png)\n\nDetermine the surface area of the above cylinder." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119719 1072 #uuid "4b99a9ed-fc2c-58e5-b129-cfd76618749f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119719 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a cylinder using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi r^2 + 2\\pi rh\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius and $h$ is the height of the cylinder.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=3 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=9\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π(3)^{2} + 2π(3)(9) \\\\\n&= 18π + 54π \\\\\n&= 72πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119720 1069 "e-8988" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119720 1071 "The radius of the base of a right cylinder is $3 \\: \\textrm{m}$ and its height is $6 \\: \\textrm{m}$. Find the lateral surface area of the cylinder." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119720 1072 #uuid "e7a7f4b8-892c-5256-a5bd-03a58936b9c1" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119720 1074 "We can calculate the lateral surface area of a cylinder using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = 2\\pi r h\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius and $h$ is the height of the cylinder.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=3 \\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=6 \\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{L} &= 2π(3)(6) = 36πm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119721 1069 "e-8989" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119721 1071 "The lateral surface area of a cylinder is $900\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^2$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $2$. Calculate the radius of the cylinder." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119721 1072 #uuid "f8ac0ac5-4cb7-57c6-b991-af55794afce6" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119721 1074 "Let $r$ and $h$ be the radius and height of the cylinder, respectively.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{r}=2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad h=2r\n$$\n\nWe can find the lateral surface area of a cylinder using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = 2\\pi rh\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values\n\n$$\nS_L=900\\pi, \\qquad h=2r\n$$\n\ninto the formula for $S_L$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{L} &= 2πrh \\\\\n900π &= 2πr(2r) \\\\\n900π &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{900π}{4π} \\\\\nr^{2} &= 225 \\\\\nr &= 15in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we disregard the negative solution since length must be positive." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119722 1069 "e-8996" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119722 1071 "![](images/fd/fd50f5ae2fda6fefd10573756f2aa27d016d23250d97d6d1cf25c7ca13ddbf08.png)\n\nThe surface area of the right cone above is $39 \\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^2$. What is the slant height of the cone?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119722 1072 #uuid "90d1cf5a-1af9-5305-827d-2530c344fcd4" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119722 1074 "We can find the surface area $S$ of a right cone using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\pi r l + \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base and $l$ is the slant height of the cone.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nS= 39 \\pi, \\qquad r=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= πrl + πr^{2} \\\\\n39π &= π(3)l + π(3)^{2} \\\\\n39π &= 3πl + 9π \\\\\n30π &= 3πl \\\\\nl &= \\frac{30π}{3π} \\\\\nl &= 10in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119723 1069 "e-8998" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119723 1071 "![](images/32/32836a9e81825bb54dc58b317a308e234bf468301b36adcfc10a65c69a4027e3.png)\n\nCalculate the surface area of the right cone shown above." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119723 1072 #uuid "cacfffd0-e197-520b-a8f9-64c18b7ba7cd" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119723 1074 "We can find the surface area $S$ of a right cone using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\pi r l + \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base and $l$ is the slant height of the cone.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=5\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nl=10\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π(5)(10) + π(5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 50π + 25π \\\\\n&= 75πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119724 1069 "e-8999" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119724 1071 "The height of a right cone is $\\sqrt7 \\: \\textrm{in}$ and the radius of its base is $3 \\: \\textrm{in}$. Find the surface area of the cone." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119724 1072 #uuid "bcb530e4-6bc5-528c-8345-6a59e7fe8da9" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119724 1074 "First, let's sketch a typical right cone and label its key features.\n\n![](images/d4/d494b2a23f36279c54ea9d0b852273c2b346f3bacfd027efc21b7f799c8a953a.png)\n\nHere, $r$ is the radius of the base, $l$ is the slant height, and $h$ is the height of the cone.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=3\\:\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh = \\sqrt7 \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{h^{2} + r^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7})^{2} + (3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the surface area of a right cone using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\pi r l + \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π(3)(4) + π(3)^{2} \\\\\n&= 12π + 9π \\\\\n&= 21πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119725 1069 "e-9004" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119725 1071 "A sphere has a diameter of $3 \\: \\textrm{in}$. Find the surface area of the sphere." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119725 1072 #uuid "fba82316-1ea7-5c90-88d7-37b96172a7f3" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119725 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi \\left(\\dfrac {3} {2} \\right)^2 = 9\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^2\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119726 1069 "e-9005" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119726 1071 "Calculate the radius of a sphere given that its surface area is $50\\pi \\: \\textrm{cm}^2$.\n\n![](images/99/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119726 1072 #uuid "e65da51f-3cfc-511a-948c-ba8a46559bba" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119726 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nS=50\\pi \\: \\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n50π &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{50π}{4π} &= r^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{25}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119727 1069 "e-9008" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119727 1071 "The volume of the sphere is $\\dfrac{500}{3}\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3$. Calculate the surface area of the sphere." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119727 1072 #uuid "acdb3e99-89dd-5463-871e-e4d5d370c827" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119727 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=\\dfrac{500}{3}\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{500}{3}π &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{500π \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 4π} &= r^{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 125 \\\\\nr &= 5in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of ​​the sphere is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n&= 4π(5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 100πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119728 1069 "e-9009" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119728 1071 "Determine the volume of the conical frustum below.\n\n![](images/c6/c684947fa0adc1b412071567c559b467c6fee3d64b7a25c950a59fc40367c101.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119728 1072 #uuid "62eb9cf6-ddad-5b9f-9b04-a1541f8eb6e3" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119728 1074 "We can find the volume of a conical frustum using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi h (R^2 + r^2 + R r)\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ and $R$ are the radii of the bases, and $h$ is the height of the frustum.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr = 2 \\: \\textrm{in}, \\qquad R = 3 \\: \\textrm{in}, \\qquad h = 4 \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{π}{3}(4)(3^{2} + 2^{2} + 3 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}(4)(19) \\\\\n&= \\frac{76π}{3}in^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119729 1069 "e-9010" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119729 1071 "Find the surface area of the conical frustum below.\n\n![](images/33/33cd2a781a4baa344d959b5be18fb4c7193b3b1818e99091ae5e8d67e454beb3.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119729 1072 #uuid "76eadde2-2ff6-5c98-ae68-5c2915ef579f" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119729 1074 "We can find the surface area of a conical frustum using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\pi \\big[ (R+r)l + R^2 + r^2 \\big]\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ and $R$ are the radii of the bases, and $l$ is the slant height of the frustum.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e68aeb05f66ad640c6f26b11afef45874849aefcd3a273e15918c40b98cc7.png)\n\nFirst, we will find the slant height of the frustum using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle shown above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{(R - r)^{2} + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(10 - 4)^{2} + (8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\nr = 4 \\: \\textrm{cm}, \\qquad R = 10 \\: \\textrm{cm}, \\qquad l = 10 \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π[(10 + 4)(10) + (10)^{2} + (4)^{2}] \\\\\n&= π[140 + 100 + 16] \\\\\n&= 256πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119730 1069 "e-9011" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119730 1071 "![](images/76/7678e1f8fd8b9cac068bbcf9deb47531e2131d5297364ca87d1a5d35cdfdb32a.png)\n\nFind the lateral surface area of the conical frustum above." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119730 1072 #uuid "ef7373d0-7b74-5f8a-a111-40b31469170a" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119730 1074 "We can find the lateral surface area of a conical frustum using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = \\pi(R + r)l\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ and $R$ are the radii of the bases, and $l$ is the slant height of the frustum.\n\n![](images/de/decdd6b63ac01dcacf52557cd660a155d00ceb572203e48e5a378732505213bf.png)\n\nFirst, we will find the slant height of the frustum using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle shown above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{(R - r)^{2} + h^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(15 - 7)^{2} + 15^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{289} \\\\\n&= 17in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\nr = 7\\: \\textrm{in}, \\qquad R = 15\\: \\textrm{in}, \\qquad l = 17 \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the slant height formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{L} &= π(R + r)l \\\\\n&= π(15 + 7)(17) \\\\\n&= 374πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119731 1069 "e-9045" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119731 1071 "Calculate the slant height of a right cone whose base radius and height are $8 \\: \\textrm{in}$ and $15 \\: \\textrm{in}$, respectively." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119731 1072 #uuid "56260914-7c49-55ba-9d68-78d2596603a3" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119731 1074 "Let $r$, $h$, and $l$ denote the cone's base radius, height, and slant height, respectively.\n\n![](images/79/79693d74e52ad2e488f5550575ce8945bd4084c165a150932ad5b592e5362521.png)\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nr=8\\,\\textrm{in}, h = 15\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nand $l$ is to be found.\n\nWe can find the slant height $l$ of the cone using the Pythagorean theorem on the right triangle shown above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{h^{2} + r^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(15)^{2} + (8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{289} \\\\\n&= 17in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119732 1069 "e-9046" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119732 1071 "The circumference of the base of the right cone shown below is $28 \\pi \\text{cm}$. What is the height of the cone?\n\n![](images/e9/e9fd558565b4f3406ee401620c3774810e2b522b6d3f560851e1bfa6cc9e4187.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119732 1072 #uuid "8c6e812f-a3df-56fc-97e3-f32e0624f187" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119732 1074 "Let $r$, $h$, and $l$ denote the cone's base radius, height, and slant height, respectively.\n\n![](images/42/420a62137384fe7fc2e7819f065ee28ccae3879e259eef50289b36bccce5919f.png)\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nl = 50\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nand $r$ and $h$ are to be found. Additionally, we're given that\n\n$$\nC=28 \\pi\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circumference of the base.\n\nThe circumference of the base is given by the formula\n\n$$\nC = 2 \\pi r\n$$\n\nSubstitutuing\n\n$$\nC = 28 \\pi \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the above formula, we can solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2πr &= 28π \\\\\nr &= \\frac{28π}{2π} \\\\\nr &= 14cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the height $h$ of the cone using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle shown in the diagram, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\sqrt{l^{2} - r^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(50)^{2} - (14)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2500 - 196} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2304} \\\\\n&= 48cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119733 1069 "e-9047" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119733 1071 "The height of a particular right cone is $\\dfrac 43$ times the radius of its base. If the volume of the cone is $12 \\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3$, what is the slant height of the cone?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119733 1072 #uuid "a47bb9fc-847e-589b-b763-5c8122f54e35" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119733 1074 "First, we are given that\n\n$$\nh=\\dfrac 43r\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the cone's base radius, and $h$ is its height.\n\nWe can find the volume of the cone using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV = 12 \\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nh=\\dfrac 43 r\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we can solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n12π &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}(\\frac{4}{3}r) \\\\\n12π &= \\frac{4}{9}πr^{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{12π \\cdot 9}{4π} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 27 \\\\\nr &= 3in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nh = \\dfrac 43r = \\dfrac 43 \\cdot 3 = 4 \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\n![](images/7f/7f69ff63b0c4be34d428f633b1cab2b49260d8e93790bdc6eed940eb7633ef0e.png)\n\nNow, we can find the slant height $l$ of the cone using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle shown above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\sqrt{h^{2} + r^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} + 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119734 1069 "e-9383" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119734 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x^3-x^5$ is an even function\n2. $\\sin^2 x$ is an even function\n3. $(x^3-x^5) \\sin^2 x$ is an odd function" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119734 1072 #uuid "2a844154-9749-5d71-9dc9-7069dcd88f7a" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119734 1074 "First, let's recall the following rules regarding the products of even and odd functions:\n\n$$\neven \\cdot even = even; odd \\cdot odd = even; even \\cdot odd = odd; odd \\cdot even = odd\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $f(x) = x^3-x^5$. Then, $f(x)$ is odd since\n$f(-x)|= (-x)^{3} - (-x)^{5}; =-x^{3} + x^{5}; = - (x^{3} - x^{5}); =-f(x)$\nfor all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x) = \\sin^2 x$. Then, $g(x)$ is even since\n$g(-x)|= \\sin^{2} (-x); = (-\\sin x)^{2}; = \\sin^{2} x; = g(x)$\nfor all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that\n$(x^{3} - x^{5}) \\cdot \\sin^{2} x|= f(x) \\cdot g(x); = odd \\cdot even; = odd$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119735 1069 "e-9385" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119735 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be a non-zero odd function, $g(x)$ be a non-zero even function, and $h(x)$ be a non-zero odd function. Is the function $f(x) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot h(x)$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119735 1072 #uuid "0889a524-e708-5dca-8cb8-a11d53cc76ca" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119735 1074 "First, let's recall the following rules regarding the products of even and odd functions:\n\n$$\neven \\cdot even = even; odd \\cdot odd = even; even \\cdot odd = odd; odd \\cdot even = odd\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot h(x) &= odd \\cdot even \\cdot odd \\\\\n&= (odd \\cdot even) \\cdot odd \\\\\n&= odd \\cdot odd \\\\\n&= even\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\cdot g(x) \\cdot h(x)$ is an even function." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119736 1069 "e-9386" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119736 1071 "Let $f(x) \\cdot x^3$ be an odd function. Is the non-zero function $f(x)$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119736 1072 #uuid "09c61322-c405-5c32-8121-8dc3eb0e5345" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119736 1074 "First, let's recall the following rules regarding the products of the non-zero even and odd functions:\n\n$$\neven \\cdot even = even; odd \\cdot odd = even; even \\cdot odd = odd; odd \\cdot even = odd\n$$\n\nNotice that $x^3$ is an odd function since\n\n$$\n(-x)^3 = -x^3\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, since $f(x) \\cdot x^3$ and $x^3$ are odd, we have that $f(x)$ must be an even function:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\text{even}}_{f(x)} \\quad \\cdot \\quad \\underbrace{\\text{odd}}_{{\\large x^3}} \\quad = \\quad \\underbrace{\\text{odd}}_{f(x) \\,\\cdot \\,{\\large x^3}}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119737 1069 "e-9390" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119737 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a Platonic solid?\n\n1. Tetrahedron\n2. Rectangular pyramid\n3. Octahedron" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119737 1072 #uuid "6cd6f989-364e-573d-8a7b-9298b98865f3" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119737 1074 "Among the given options, only the *rectangular pyramid* is not a Platonic solid.\n\nRecall that there are only five types of Platonic solids (convex regular polyhedra):\n\n![](images/bc/bc67deabe1622e7ac3015c3ec942f254e59c58f406e026c367e0c0aeee5a6d3e.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119738 1069 "e-9402" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119738 1071 "![](images/8f/8f545282537781d088d76c2b73e387ebcc7cddaa6e518d9cb2b4f6fa263da024.png)\n\nWhat regular polyhedron is shown in the picture above?\n\n1. Dodecahedron\n2. Icosahedron\n3. Tetrahedron" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119738 1072 #uuid "ff8b375f-f8cf-5f40-a6b0-c0f1ea81e716" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119738 1074 "The picture shows a *tetrahedron*, a convex regular polyhedron that\n\n- consists of $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, and\n- exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex.\n\nRecall that there are only five types of Platonic solids (convex regular polyhedra):\n\n![](images/bc/bc67deabe1622e7ac3015c3ec942f254e59c58f406e026c367e0c0aeee5a6d3e.png)" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119739 1069 "e-9404" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119739 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true regarding any tetrahedron?\n\n1. It has $6$ faces\n2. It has $6$ edges\n3. It has $4$ vertices" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119739 1072 #uuid "d45623b8-26bf-5925-8400-08e25749b5ca" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119739 1074 "Below is an example of a tetrahedron.\n\n![](images/ad/adcc202ad86b782fd153566d6454269dcd50e8f1d9aca37bc42d2266c142ced8.png)\n\nLet's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. A tetrahedron is a regular polyhedron that consists of $4$ congruent faces, each in the form of an equilateral triangle, and exactly $3$ faces meet at each vertex.\n- Statement II is true. Since there are $F=4$ faces, each in the form of a triangle, we have\n$S = 4 \\cdot 3 = 12$\nsides combined. Now, each edge of the polyhedron is created when two face sides coincide. So, the number of edges of the tetrahedron must be equal to\n$\\dfrac{S}{2} = \\dfrac{12}{2} = 6$.\n- Statement III is true. Substituting the number of edges $E=6$ and the number of faces $F=4$ into Euler's formula, we can solve for the number of vertices $V$ as follows:\n$V - E + F|= 2; V - (6) + (4)|= 2; V - 2|= 2; V|= 4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119740 1069 "e-9852" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119740 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}$?\n\n1. The function is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=e^{-x}$.\n2. The function is decreasing over $(-\\infty,0)$ and increasing over $(0, \\infty)$.\n3. The function is odd." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119740 1072 #uuid "1d35d432-14f0-53b6-9fa1-8d51cb5bfefb" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119740 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\sinh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/be/bed96bcd7370c5e66c9cfb8137199a3dbc39ac5250f325f61e10190be3ef1e25.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is the average of $y=e^x$ and $y=-e^{-x}{:}$\n$\\sinh{x} = \\dfrac{e^x+\\left(-e^{-x}\\right)}{2}= \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{2}$\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is increasing over the entire real line.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\sinh{x}$ is odd, i.e., $\\sinh(-x)=-\\sinh(x)$, and the graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119741 1069 "e-9853" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119741 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}$?\n\n1. The domain of the function is $[0, \\infty)$.\n2. The graph of the function is symmetric with respect to the $y$-axis.\n3. The minimum of the function occurs at $x=0$." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119741 1072 #uuid "e4227bdb-e78f-59e2-a925-b25ca204f9e0" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119741 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/bd/bdcc58c5097062db8cbe5875a4af6f91db08f7e7c61a264f15f612f77d0e3350.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The domain of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $[1,\\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is even, i.e. $\\cosh(-x)=\\cosh(x)$, and the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the minimum value of the function $y=\\cosh{x}$ is $y=1$ and occurs at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119742 1069 "e-9854" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119742 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function\n$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}$?\n\n1. $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n3. The function has no zeros." 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119742 1072 #uuid "794c3065-11b6-58fb-874d-31b142ea04b1" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119742 1074 "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} y=\\tanh{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fa55375a8d37d72a82600c3d0ad4466578b67c919eef756d1abddc6ab9535e7f.png)\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that $\\tanh{x} \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$.\n- Statement II is false. The range of $\\tanh{x}$ is $(-1,1)$.\n- Statement III is false. The function $y=\\tanh{x}$ has a zero at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533357 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120233 131941395